    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>4. RESP grant</h2>
      <p>An RESP plan shows <strong>CESG</strong> as <strong>Expected</strong>, <strong>Received</strong>, and <strong>Not yet received</strong>. Those are estimates. The screen says to confirm residency and the SIN with the plan promoter. It is not tax advice.</p>
      <p>The app can suggest a contribution that would capture more grant this year. That suggestion stops once the child is past the grant age the app tracks.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/guides/registered-resp.jpg" width="800" height="1739" alt="An RESP plan, with CESG listed as Expected, Received, and Not yet received.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/guides/registered-resp-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1739" alt="An RESP plan, with CESG listed as Expected, Received, and Not yet received.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>5. Withdrawal tax estimate</h2>
  <p>On an RRSP account, <strong>Estimate tax on withdrawal</strong> uses the person’s rate under <strong>Manage People</strong>. The starting rate is a planning placeholder. The account editor says it is not your tax rate and not tax advice. A TFSA or an RESP does not use that estimate.</p>
